From: bigjakk <6015188+bigjakk@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 20 Nov 2026 12:00:00 -0500
Subject: kcc: coalesce same-time setTargetAtTime events in AudioParam timelines

Stock behaviour (third_party/blink/renderer/modules/webaudio/
audio_param_handler.cc, AudioParamHandler::InsertEvent):

Howler 2.2.3 implements Howler.pos()/orientation() as nine
AudioParam.setTargetAtTime(v, ctx.currentTime, 0.1) calls on the
AudioListener params, and Krunker calls them every frame.  currentTime
only advances once per 128-frame render quantum and not at all while the
context is suspended (Howler suspends it after 30 s of silence), so many
consecutive calls insert an event at exactly the same time.

 - Each call appends one ParamEvent.  The overlap scan
   ("for (int i = ub; i >= 0; i--)") only stops at
   "if (events_[i]->Time() < insert_time) break;", so it walks every
   event that shares the new event's time: the N-th call costs N pointer
   chases, N^2 in total.
 - Events are only removed by the audio thread (RemoveOldEvents from
   ValuesForFrameRangeImpl and HandleAllEventsInThePast), by the prune
   loop at the top of InsertEvent, and by the cancel calls.  On a
   running context AudioListenerHandler::UpdateState() evaluates the
   listener params every quantum, so the lists stay at a handful of
   events.  On a suspended context the audio thread does not run, and
   the prune loop uses IsEventCurrent(), i.e.
   "next_event->Time() * sample_rate >= current_frame".  For same-time
   events that is true unless (F / rate) * rate rounds to less than F,
   which happens for about 8% of quantum-aligned frames at 44.1 and
   48 kHz.  For the other 92% (and always at frame 0, i.e. before the
   context first starts) nothing is pruned.
 - So while suspended each of the nine lists (and new_events_) grows by
   one event per call for as long as the game runs, every call gets
   slower in proportion, memory grows by roughly 100+ bytes per event,
   and on resume the audio thread has to clamp, stable_sort and free the
   whole list under events_lock_.  This is the frame rate that decays
   over a couple of minutes in the menu.

Change (feature KccAudioParamCoalesce, disabled by default):

When a SetTarget event is inserted at the end of the list and the three
events before it are SetTargets at exactly the same time, the middle one
of those three is erased before the new event is appended.  A same-time
run therefore holds at most its first event and its last two, and the
per-call cost is constant.  The dropped event always sits between two
SetTargets that start at its own time, so it has zero duration.

It is restricted to the case that could be checked:
 - the new event is a SetTarget and goes at the end of the list;
 - its time is <= the context's current time (scheduled for "now");
 - the dropped event and its successor are still in new_events_ (the
   audio thread has not run the timeline since they were inserted);
 - every event in the list is a SetTarget, and the list holds <= 32.
Nothing else is coalesced: not SetValue/SetValue, not SetTarget followed
by SetValue, nothing involving ramps, curves, or the synthetic
kCancelValues / kSetValueCurveEnd events.  The last event at a given
time is never the one dropped.

Why first + last two rather than "replace the previous event":
 - When (F / rate) * rate rounds to more than F (also ~8% of frames),
   ValuesForFrameRangeImpl gives the first event of a run one frame
   (one k-rate step in AudioParamHandler::Value()) before the last one
   takes over.
 - CancelAndHoldAtTime(t) with t equal to the run's time removes only
   the last SetTarget and leaves the one before it running.
 - ProcessSetTargetFollowedByRamp rewrites the last one in place.

What is not identical to stock:
 - Float rounding (the only difference the intended workload can show).
   When a zero-length SetTarget is processed at its own start frame,
   ProcessSetTarget still executes
   "value = target + (value - target) * exp(-0 / tau)".  That is a
   mathematical no-op, but (value - target) is rounded to float, so each
   such event can move value by up to 2^-24 * |value - target| (not at
   all when value and target are within a factor of two of each other).
   Stock does this for every stacked event, the patch for at most two,
   so the patched result is the one closer to the specified curve.
 - cancelAndHoldAtTime() at exactly the run's time, called twice with no
   setTargetAtTime in between, after at least four same-time
   setTargetAtTime calls: stock leaves the third-from-last target
   running, the patch the first.  (The spec says hold.)  A single such
   call leaves the same target running as stock.
 - RemoveOldEvents(skipped_count) removes the first N events whichever
   ones were skipped (existing behaviour).  In a SetTarget-only timeline
   at the current time the skipped events are a prefix and nothing
   differs.  If other automation (ramps, set-values, future events, a
   cancelAndHoldAtTime at the run's time) is added to the same param
   before the audio thread next runs it, that prefix can cover
   different events in the shorter list.  In the model below this only
   showed up as a one-quantum difference in a second evaluation of the
   param within the same quantum.
The last two cannot happen on a param that only ever receives
setTargetAtTime(v, currentTime, tau), which is what Howler does to the
listener.

Verification: nothing was compiled.  The timeline code was ported by
hand to a JS model (float via Math.fround, x86 and non-x86 paths,
without SetValueCurve) and the stock and patched lists were driven with
the same random call/render sequences.  With the re-rounding step
idealised away the outputs were bit-identical over 8000 random
SetTarget-only sequences (2.2M dropped events); with real float
arithmetic a realistic listener workload differed by at most 1.1e-6
(position, world units) and 5.4e-7 (direction component).  A model is
not the real code: treat this as evidence, not proof.

Threading: InsertEvent runs on the main thread with events_lock_ held
(EXCLUSIVE_LOCKS_REQUIRED); the audio thread only touches events_ /
new_events_ under AutoTryLock and keeps no index or pointer into them
across a lock release.  The erased event's pointer is removed from
new_events_ first.

A/B: --enable-features=KccAudioParamCoalesce
 - Leave the game where the AudioContext is suspended (30+ s without
   sound, or before the first click) and log frame time for 3 minutes:
   stock climbs steadily, patched stays flat.  If stock stays flat the
   suspend landed on one of the ~8% self-pruning frames; resume and
   suspend again.
 - Renderer private bytes over the same period (stock grows by roughly
   1 MB/s at 1000 fps).
 - Hitch on the first sound after a long suspend.
 - Deterministic check from devtools: suspend an AudioContext, then
   time batches of 1000 x listener.positionX.setTargetAtTime(
   Math.random(), ctx.currentTime, 0.1); stock time per batch grows
   linearly with the number of batches, patched is constant.

Platforms: all (no platform-specific code).

Not verified: compilation; whether Krunker touches the listener params
in any other way (a SetValue or ramp event left in a listener timeline
disables coalescing for that param until it is pruned).

Build and check status (2026-11-21)
  - Compiled without any change on Windows, in the Electron 45.4.1 DCHECK
    build (dcheck_always_on) and in the official Release build (dist.zip).
  - DCHECK build: test/page-check.js + audioparam.html: with the switch the
    time to queue a batch of setTargetAtTime events stays flat while the
    AudioContext is suspended (0.50-1.02x over four scenarios); with it off it
    grows 12-13x.
  - Release build (dist.zip): with the switch 0.47-1.00x over the four
    scenarios; off 14-15x.
  - Not checked: whether Krunker hits this path; the rounding difference
    against stock (by design not bit-identical).
The "not compiled" / "not verified" notes above were written before the
build; they stay for the caveats they list. Details and raw logs:
patches/electron-45/RESULTS.md and results/.

diff --git a/third_party/blink/renderer/modules/webaudio/audio_param_handler.cc b/third_party/blink/renderer/modules/webaudio/audio_param_handler.cc
index 2f4828436507e91b94e5466e4bb5e6407337f529..20991d915769d6e82bfd78da3fe81cfc86002d25 100644
--- a/third_party/blink/renderer/modules/webaudio/audio_param_handler.cc
+++ b/third_party/blink/renderer/modules/webaudio/audio_param_handler.cc
@@ -10,6 +10,7 @@
 
 #include "base/check_op.h"
 #include "base/containers/span.h"
+#include "base/feature_list.h"
 #include "base/memory/ptr_util.h"
 #include "build/build_config.h"
 #include "media/base/audio_bus.h"
@@ -47,6 +48,23 @@ namespace {
 constexpr float kTimeConstantsToConverge = 10.0f;
 constexpr float kSetTargetThreshold = 4.539992976248485e-05f;
 
+// KCC: when enabled, `InsertEvent()` keeps a run of setTargetAtTime events that
+// all start at the same, already reached, time down to three events (the
+// first and the last two) instead of letting it grow by one event per call.
+// See the comment in `InsertEvent()`.
+BASE_FEATURE(kKccAudioParamCoalesce, base::FEATURE_DISABLED_BY_DEFAULT);
+
+// `InsertEvent()` only coalesces while the timeline holds no more than this
+// many events, which bounds the extra work per call.  A timeline that is being
+// coalesced stays far below this.
+constexpr wtf_size_t kKccCoalesceMaxEvents = 32;
+
+bool IsKccAudioParamCoalesceEnabled() {
+  static const bool enabled =
+      base::FeatureList::IsEnabled(kKccAudioParamCoalesce);
+  return enabled;
+}
+
 // Replace NaN values in `values` with `default_value`.
 void HandleNaNValues(base::span<float> values, float default_value) {
   unsigned k = 0;
@@ -1004,6 +1022,54 @@ bool AudioParamHandler::InsertEvent(std::unique_ptr<ParamEvent> event,
     }
   }
 
+  // KccAudioParamCoalesce.  A page that calls setTargetAtTime(v, currentTime,
+  // tau) once per animation frame passes the same time many times, because
+  // currentTime only advances once per render quantum and not at all while the
+  // context is suspended.  Every such call appends an event, and the scan above
+  // walks all the events that share the new event's time.
+  //
+  // When the three events that would precede the new one are all SetTarget
+  // events at exactly the new event's time, the middle one of those three is
+  // dropped: it sits between two SetTargets that start at its own time, so the
+  // timeline can never run it for any length of time.  A run therefore keeps
+  // its first event and its last two.  Those are the ones that the rest of
+  // this file can tell apart from an interior event: the first event of a run
+  // can own the first frame of a render quantum in
+  // `ValuesForFrameRangeImpl()`, `CancelAndHoldAtTime()` removes only the last
+  // SetTarget at the cancel time and so re-exposes the one before it, and
+  // `ProcessSetTargetFollowedByRamp()` rewrites the last one.
+  //
+  // This is only done when
+  //  - the time has already been reached, so that nothing can later be
+  //    inserted in front of the run and the audio thread first sees the run
+  //    at the start of a render quantum;
+  //  - the event to drop and its successor are still in `new_events_`, i.e.
+  //    the audio thread has not run the timeline since they were inserted, so
+  //    they have rendered nothing and will be clamped to the same time;
+  //  - the new event goes at the end of the list and every event already in
+  //    the list is a SetTarget, so that the events the audio thread skips form
+  //    a prefix of the list, which is what `RemoveOldEvents()` assumes.
+  if (IsKccAudioParamCoalesceEnabled() &&
+      event->GetType() == ParamEvent::Type::kSetTarget &&
+      insertion_idx == events_.size() && insertion_idx >= 3 &&
+      insertion_idx <= kKccCoalesceMaxEvents &&
+      insert_time <= DestinationHandler().CurrentTime()) {
+    ParamEvent* const previous = events_[insertion_idx - 1].get();
+    ParamEvent* const interior = events_[insertion_idx - 2].get();
+    bool can_coalesce =
+        previous->Time() == insert_time && interior->Time() == insert_time &&
+        events_[insertion_idx - 3]->Time() == insert_time &&
+        new_events_.Contains(previous) && new_events_.Contains(interior);
+    for (wtf_size_t i = 0; can_coalesce && i < insertion_idx; ++i) {
+      can_coalesce = events_[i]->GetType() == ParamEvent::Type::kSetTarget;
+    }
+    if (can_coalesce) {
+      new_events_.erase(interior);
+      events_.EraseAt(insertion_idx - 2);
+      --insertion_idx;
+    }
+  }
+
   events_.insert(insertion_idx, std::move(event));
   new_events_.insert(events_[insertion_idx].get());
   return true;
